===Glyph origin===
{{Han etym}}
Li Shoukui (2016): {{Han compound|聿|規|ls=psc|c1=s|c2=p|t1=brush}}. {{och-l|*規|compass}} appears in the oracle bone script in its pictographic protoform resembling a {{och-l|*乂}}. During the Western Zhou period, [[周]] was added as seen in the bronze inscriptions.

Traditionally explained as an ideogrammic compound of <span class="Hani" lang="zh">[[聿]]</span> ("brush") + <span class="Hani" lang="zh">[[画]]</span> ("a field ([[田]]) enclosed on four sides"). However, the ancient scripts do not depict a field.

{{wp|Stroke (CJK character)}}

===Etymology===
{{och-l|畫|tr= {{IPAfont|*C-ɡʷˤrek-s}}|drawing}} is [[exopassive]] derivation of {{och-l|畫|tr= {{IPAfont|*ɡʷˤrek}}|to draw}} ({{zh-ref|Schuessler, 2007}}; {{zh-ref|Baxter & Sagart, 2014}}). Schuessler additionally relates {{zh-l|*畫}} to {{zh-l|劃|tr= huá ~ huà}} < {{och-l|劃|tr= {{IPAfont|*ɡʷˤrek}}}} "to plan, to delineate" < "to mark off" ({{w|Zuozhuan}}), "to cut open" ({{zh-ref|Wang, 1982}}) < "to cut (with a knife)" ({{w|Shuowen Jiezi|SWJZ}}) ([[ibid.]]).

For more, see {{zh-l|劃}}.

